# FreeSail: the starter book (spec M4 §3 and §6; package 33c, decision 30).
#
# The general and instructive routines of a ship at sea, each with the reason it exists:
# the night and the morning sail, shortening sail for weather, keeping her full, trimming
# on a shift, tending the sheets, heavy weather and the storm staysail, and the well. It is
# a choice, never a requirement for beginning (the owner's ruling at gate 5b): a game
# begins with an empty book unless its scenario names this file (the gates' days and
# passages do) or the player asks for it, and a passage's own orders are in the
# scenario's file beside it (data/scenarios/*.orders). The primer's chapter 11, "The
# starting book" (docs/primer/11-the-starting-book.md), prints each routine with its
# reason and shows how to write one's own.
#
# Standing orders in the standing dialect, one a line; a line beginning # is a
# comment, and a sentence may run on to indented lines. Load them at the prompt with
#     read the standing orders from data/standing_orders/starter.orders
# or at the start with
#     py -m freesail.ui.console data/ships/frigate-36.yaml --standing-orders data/standing_orders/starter.orders
#     py -m freesail.ui.server data/ships/frigate-36.yaml --standing-orders data/standing_orders/starter.orders
# Each line is given as the captain's order, journaled, and replays; one the ship refuses
# is logged as refused and the rest still load.

# The night routine. The light sails come in for the dark hours: Luce's routine of the day
# has the topgallant yards sent down at sunset (Luce 1866, ch. XXXII, port routine, art.
# XXVI: "At sunset, hoist boats and send down top-gallant yards") and hammocks piped down
# at sunset at sea (the same, daily routine at sea, dog watch, art. IV); before a squall
# "furl the topgallant sails and royals, and stow the light sails" (Luce 1884, ch. XXV).
# At sea, with the yards kept across, the game's version takes in the studding sails and
# the royals (judgement: the light sails a watch cannot see to handle in the dark).
standing order "night routine": at sunset then take in the studdingsails; take in the royals

# The morning sail. The reverse of the night routine at daylight (Luce 1866, ch. XXXII,
# port routine: "At early daylight ... call all hands", and the yards crossed again), if
# the breeze allows: under 20 knots is the wind in which truth 9 (tests/test_known_truths.py)
# and the strain tables carry every sail without a strain line; over it the royals stay in.
standing order "morning sail": at sunrise, if the true wind is under 20 knots then set the royals

# Shortening sail for weather. Thirty knots is where the strain truths begin to bite: in
# 35 knots the royals and topgallants carry away within twenty minutes under all sail
# (truth 9), and docs/dev/TuningNotes.md records the light sails' ratings tuned to that;
# two minutes is the duration that lets a gust pass (spec §3's debounce; the M2 wind's
# gusts last five to thirty seconds, physics/wind.py). The topgallants come in with the
# royals (owner's ruling at gate 4c, 2026-09-28, from package 29's first runs of the gate's
# day, in which the frigate carried her topgallants into the rising wind and blew two out
# at 35 knots): Luce's order of shortening sail as it freshens is royals, topgallants,
# then the reefs, and Beaufort's force 7 (28 to 33 knots) carries "double-reefed topsails
# and jib", the topgallant sails in.
standing order "shorten sail for weather": when the true wind exceeds 30 knots for 2 minutes
    then take in the studdingsails; take in the royals; take in the topgallants; reef the topsails, one reef

# Keeping her full. Fifty-five degrees of apparent wind is a little forward of where the
# frigate's sails just fill: on a wind she carries the apparent wind at 48 to 52 degrees
# (truth 1's sweeps and the primer's first passage), and the pointing truths (25, 26, 32)
# put her closest holding way near 52 to 56 degrees off the true wind. Forward of 55
# apparent she is pinching, and a point off brings her back to a good full.
standing order "keep her full": when the apparent wind is forward of 55 degrees then bear away one point

# Trimming on a shift. The yards are trimmed to the wind as it shifts: Luce's table of the
# best angle of the yard with the keel is given point by point of the wind (Luce 1866,
# ch. XXIV, 'To Trim Yards'), so a point is the shift a trim answers. The order stands
# again once the wind has held its new direction for five minutes, and measures the next
# point from there, so a wind that veers steadily from west to north-west through a night
# is trimmed to point by point (playtest 7: the yards stayed braced for the old wind until
# the captain asked). A gust does not move the wind's direction; its wander about the base
# is a few degrees either way (three in a warm sector, five in neutral air, eight in the
# unstable air behind a cold front, physics/wind.py, since package 31b under every wind),
# so a shift of a point is mostly the shift and not the wander that fires it, though the
# wander moves the firing some minutes and in unstable air may fire it by itself now and
# then; a trim the watch is still at is not stacked behind, the yards still to brace take
# the new angle (orders/verbs.py).
# The guard is the dialect's own (package 33c) and was put here by package 37f: while she
# is hove to her yards are set against each other on purpose and are not trimmed (in two
# playtests this rule braced a hove-to ship's backed yards round and filled her, four
# times). `trim sails` itself declines while she lies to; with the guard the rule sleeps
# and logs no refusal, and a shift that came while she lay to is trimmed to when she has
# filled away.
standing order "trim on a shift": when the true wind veers 1 point or backs 1 point
    and the manoeuvre in hand is not hove to then trim sails

# Tending the sheets. Since package 32e the sheet holds the trim (spec M5 open item 13):
# a jib's or a spanker's sheet stays where it was worked until hands work it again, so
# the afterguard's routine of tending the fore-and-aft sheets to the wind is the book's,
# every glass (the half-hour the sand runs), as sounding the well is; "trim on a shift"
# above tends them with the yards when the wind shifts a point. A sheet within a degree
# of its trim stands, and the line says so; a ship whose hands are all aloft has sheets
# that are not tended, which is true. Luce 1866, ch. XXIV, 'Tacking': "trim aft the
# sheets"; the cadence is judgement. Not while she is hove to (package 37f): the watch
# then tends the spanker and jib sheets to keep her head where it lies, not to the wind.
standing order "tend the sheets": every glass, if the manoeuvre in hand is not hove to then trim the sheets

# Heavy weather. Forty knots is a gale (units.describe_wind_strength); in 45 knots the
# storm canvas of truth 28 has the topgallant masts sent down, the storm staysails bent and
# the topsails close-reefed, and nothing carries away in an hour. Five minutes is the
# duration that tells a gale from a squall (spec §3; judgement). The fore storm staysail
# sets on the fore stay beside the fore topmast staysail's (Luce 1884 ch. XXIX 'Reducing
# Sail to a Gale'), so it is bent from the sail room and set as a sail of its own; the
# setting waits for the bending in the companion order below. In a gale that rises faster
# than the head sails are shortened the fore topmast staysail may have blown out before
# this fires (it goes at about 43 knots under plain sail on a wind); the order is then
# refused in words and the rest go on.
# The order of the clauses is the order of the work (package 31b, from playtest 11's
# finding 10): the hands go to the first clause first, and the rest get what is left and
# what comes free. The close reef leads: the three topsails are reefed together by three
# parties of forty (spec M3 §3.2) while the sending down of the masts, which takes every
# hand it can get, begins short and fills as the reefs are done. With the send-down first,
# the reefs waited for its hands and the topsails were still unreefed when the first squall
# of the middle watch came on the day under systems (docs/dev/TuningNotes.md, M5a, package
# 31b); Luce's order of shortening sail is the reefs before the light spars come down
# ("If the wind still increases ... take a second and a third reef", then "it is
# recommended to send down top-gallant masts in a heavy gale", Luce 1884, ch. XXVII, XXIX).
standing order "heavy weather": when the true wind exceeds 40 knots for 5 minutes
    then close reef the topsails;
    send down the topgallant masts;
    take in the fore topmast staysail;
    bend the fore storm staysail

# The storm staysail, set when it is bent: a standing order may test a sail's state, and
# "bent and furled" is what the bending leaves. Edge-triggered, so it fires once as the
# sail comes to be furled with the gale still blowing.
standing order "storm staysail": when the fore storm staysail is furled and the true wind exceeds 40 knots
    then set the fore storm staysail

# Sounding the well every glass is the pump routine of Luce's day ("Pump the ship out",
# Luce 1866, ch. XXXII, daily routine at sea, morning watch art. III and dog watch art.
# III). The ship has no well yet (spec M4 §24 item 4; milestone 5 brought the glass and
# the chart, not the well), so this order is entered in the
# book and held, saying "The ship has no well to sound yet", and it never fires until the
# well is a reading (package 33c; before, it was refused at every start, a line of noise
# in every log since milestone 4a); it is kept here so that the book is complete when the
# well arrives.
standing order "sound the well": every glass then sound the well
